.TH ADDCCTL 1 "" "addcctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
addcctl \- operations of a Samba Active Directory domain controller, as JSON
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B addcctl
.I object action
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.RI < input.json
.br
.B addcctl schema
.br
.B addcctl mcp
.RB [ \-\-read\-only ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B addcctl
runs on a Samba AD domain controller, as root, and performs one operation:
it reads a JSON object on standard input and writes a JSON object on
standard output. It reads the configuration through Samba's loadparm and the
directory from the local sam.ldb, so it needs no domain credentials.
.PP
It is the core of cockpit-samba-dc: the Cockpit page, the Ansible collection
lineadicomando.samba_dc and the MCP server call it.
.PP
.B addcctl schema
prints every operation with the JSON Schema of its input.
.PP
.B addcctl mcp
is an MCP (Model Context Protocol) server on standard input and output, one
JSON-RPC message per line: one tool per operation, named
.IR object _ action
(dashes become underscores), with the operation's input. A tool that changes
something also takes "check": true, the preview of
.BR \-\-check .
Every change is written to syslog (facility authpriv, tag addcctl-mcp) with
the user who started the server and the passwords masked. It is meant to be
started by an MCP client through SSH and sudo.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report what the operation would change, in the "commands" field of the
output, and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-read\-only
With
.BR mcp ,
offer only the operations that change nothing.
.SH OPERATIONS
Names are sAMAccountNames. The input of each operation, with its fields and
their meaning, is in
.BR "addcctl schema" .
.TP
.B user list\fR, \fBuser get
The domain users, with their groups, status, lockout and expiry.
.TP
.B user create\fR, \fBuser set\fR, \fBuser password\fR, \fBuser unlock\fR, \fBuser delete
Create a user; bring it to the given settings (a new name also renames it in
the shared folders); set its password; unlock it; delete it. Passwords come
in the input and are never written out. The built-in accounts are protected.
.TP
.B group list\fR, \fBgroup get\fR, \fBgroup create\fR, \fBgroup set\fR, \fBgroup delete
The groups with their members, and their changes.
.TP
.B computer list\fR, \fBcomputer get\fR, \fBcomputer create\fR, \fBcomputer delete
The computer accounts; create one before the computer joins; delete one with
its DNS records (not a domain controller).
.TP
.B home provision\fR, \fBhome delete
A user's home directory under /srv/samba/home, mapped at logon; clear it, and
with "delete_data" remove its folder.
.TP
.B domain password\-policy
The domain password and lockout settings.
.TP
.B share list\fR, \fBshare get
The shared folders under /srv/samba/shares, with their access list and drive
mapping.
.TP
.B share set
Create a shared folder, or bring it to the given settings: name, access,
access_mode, comment, browseable, drive. Settings left out keep their value.
.TP
.B share delete
Remove a shared folder and its drive mapping; with "delete_data", its folder
too.
.TP
.B share rename\-principal
Follow the rename of a user or group in the shared folders that list it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when the operation fails; 2 for an unknown operation or an
input that is not valid. On failure the output is {"error": message} and the
message is also written to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
echo '{"name": "Projects", "access": [{"name": "Staff", "level": "write"}],
       "drive": {"letter": "P"}}' | addcctl share set \-\-check
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR samba\-tool (8),
.BR net (8)
